#!/usr/bin/env -S python3 -I
"""loglab — switch where each Fleet lab instance sends its osquery result/status logs and audit logs. See ../README.md.

  loglab                                  interactive view: instances, live vs configured destination, sinks; Enter → switch
  loglab status [--json]                  instances (up, version, Hangar wiring, configured vs live, restart needed) + sinks
  loglab path <inst>                      the config file to pass to `fleet serve --config` (created if missing)
  loglab switch <inst> <dest|default> [--only result,status,audit] [--yes]
                                          send all log types (result, status, audit) to that destination: writes
                                          config/<inst>.yml, validated with the instance's own `fleet config_dump`;
                                          you restart the server (Fleet Hangar or your own fleet serve) to apply.
                                          --only (advanced) moves just those types and leaves the others where they are
  loglab test <inst> [--no-write]         live server vs file, Hangar wiring, each destination reachable (+ a marked test
                                          record unless --no-write), log-writer errors in the server log since start
  loglab fire <inst> [--status-from <vm>] [--minutes 4] [--keep]
                                          generate logs now: a temporary global report (60 s, automations on) makes every
                                          host send result logs, creating/deleting it makes audit events; --status-from
                                          restarts osquery on that VM for status logs. Watches, then deletes the report.
  loglab fire <inst> --audit [--minutes 8]
                                          API-only audit check: create + delete a report that never runs (no hosts
                                          touched), wait for both activities at the audit destination (≤ ~6 min)
  loglab fire <inst> --cleanup            delete leftover fire reports (if a run was killed)
  loglab view <inst> [--type result,status,audit] [--raw] [--from-start] [--grep TEXT]
                                          stream the logs back from wherever they are going now (Ctrl-C to stop)
  loglab up|down <receiver|localstack|redpanda|nats|pubsub|splunk|all>
                                          start/stop a local sink (up also creates the streams/topics for every instance)
  loglab aws guard install [--dry-run] [--update]|status|run [--all] [--dry-run]|down
                                          cost safeguards, ONE per AWS account shared by every loglab user: a reaper
                                          Lambda that deletes expired loglab resources every 15 min (from inside AWS,
                                          Mac off or not), a monthly budget (email at $5, delete-all at $20) and anomaly
                                          emails. install leaves an existing guard alone; --update rolls out this reaper
  loglab aws up [kinesis|firehose|lambda] [--ttl 4h]
                                          real-AWS resources for kinesis-aws / firehose-aws / lambda-aws; refuses
                                          without the guard; tagged owner=$LOGLAB_OWNER and expires-at (default 4h, max 24h)
  loglab aws status|extend <2h>|down [--all] [--include-unowned]|login
                                          loglab resources in AWS (yours, others', unowned) and when they expire / add
                                          time / delete yours now (--all: also bucket + roles)
  loglab show <inst>                      the generated YAML (secrets masked)
  loglab env <inst>                       the same settings as FLEET_* env rows (alternative to --config)
  loglab hangar                           one-time Hangar wiring: which Config file each server must point at (n/a
                                          without Hangar: use `loglab path <inst>` with fleet serve --config)
  loglab dests                            every destination, what it needs, what it supports
  Destinations: filesystem stdout webhook splunk splunk-cloud kinesis kinesis-aws firehose firehose-aws lambda-aws
                pubsub kafkarest nats splunk-local   ("default" = no logging keys: Fleet's built-in filesystem in $TMPDIR, audit off)

Config: lab.env (INSTANCES, INSTANCE_<i>_*, LOGLAB_DATA, LOGLAB_OWNER, HANGAR); see tools/qalab/README.md.
Only writes its data dir ($LOGLAB_DATA: config/<inst>.yml, sinks/, state/); reads Fleet Hangar's settings.json,
running.json and server logs if you use Hangar, never restarts a server; docker only on compose project "loglab"
with memory-capped containers on ports Hangar and ssolab don't use.
"""
